# Direct Output in Laravel Migrations

Running migrations via Artisan is a standard workflow for seeding databases with test data, but the default execution mode often feels like a black box. You execute `php artisan migrate --seed`, and either it succeeds or it fails silently unless you check the exit code. If you are injecting custom logic into your migration files to handle complex generation tasksâlike looping through hundreds of recordsâyou need visibility into what is happening in real time. Simply using native PHP's `echo` statement, as shown in typical migration drafts, will not work because Artisan captures standard output and redirects it to its own logging mechanism or suppresses it entirely depending on the context.

To achieve console output during a migration run without cluttering your application logs, you must use Laravel's built-in `Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface` via the `info()` helper provided by the Artisan command kernel. Within a migration file, you don't have direct access to the raw output instance in the same way you do inside a CLI command. Instead, the most reliable pattern is to wrap your custom logic inside an Artisan command or use the `DB::listen` feature for query logging if your goal includes tracking SQL execution alongside progress updates.

However, if you strictly require text to appear on the terminal while the migration runs, the cleanest approach without creating a separate Command class is to utilize the `info()` method exposed through the `Illuminate\Console\Scheduling\Event` or by injecting the output interface directly into your migration if you are running it via a custom command. But there is a more direct way specifically for migration files: you can create a dedicated Artisan command that executes your migration logic manually, allowing you to access `$this->output`.

Here is how you would structure an Artisan command to handle the output correctly:

```php
use Illuminate\Console\Command;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

class SampleUserSeeder extends Command
{
    protected $signature = 'db:seed-sample-users';
    protected $description = 'Generate and insert sample users with console feedback';

    public function handle()
    {
        // Initialize your generator logic here
        $generator = new Sample_Data();
        $user_count = 30;
        $users = [];

        $this->info('Creating sample users...');

        for ($i = 0; $i < $user_count; $i++) {
            $user = $generator->generate_user($i);

            // Optional: Show progress every few iterations
            if ($i % 5 === 0) {
                $this->line("Processed user #{$i}");
            }

            array_push($users, $user);
        }

        DB::table('users')->insert($users);

        $this->info("Successfully inserted {$user_count} users.");

        return Command::SUCCESS;
    }
}
```

Once the command is registered in your `ConsoleKernel` or via a simple artisan call, you can run it with `php artisan db:seed-sample-users`. This approach ensures that every line printed using `$this->info()` or `$this->line()` appears directly on your terminal stream.

If you absolutely must keep the logic inside a migration file and cannot create a separate command, you are limited by how Artisan executes migrations. Standard `php artisan migrate` does not expose the output interface to the closure within the migration class. Consequently, attempting to `echo` inside the `up()` method will result in no visible output on the console. The trade-off here is architectural: if your seeding logic is too complex to fit neatly into a standard migration, extracting it into an Artisan command is the idiomatic Laravel solution. This separation keeps migrations focused on schema changes and ensures that data generation steps remain observable and debuggable via CLI tools.
